         <span class="block-title">Definition 6.1 <span class="name">(primitives)</span></span>
-        <p>There is one risky asset. Its payoff is a random variable \(v\) with prior variance \(\sigma_v^2\). A <dfn>signal</dfn> is \(s = v + \varepsilon\), where \(\varepsilon\) is the signal noise with variance \(\sigma_\varepsilon^2\). A <dfn>noise trader</dfn> is an investor whose trade is not a function of the payoff information. In this model the noise traders are represented by a random net supply \(u\) of the asset: \(u > 0\) means that noise traders sell \(u\) units to the market, and \(u < 0\) means that they buy. The variance of \(u\) is \(\sigma_u^2\). The <dfn>precisions</dfn> are the reciprocals \(\tau_v = 1/\sigma_v^2\) and \(\tau_\varepsilon = 1/\sigma_\varepsilon^2\). The informed fraction is \(\lambda\), and the CARA coefficient is \(a\).</p>
